11/20, 11/15 (1970) KA11 processor, generally shipped with 4 or 8kW Core
             memory. Some systems had a whopping 28kW! I/O generally via LT33
             Teletype @30cps and Papertape reader/punch @300cps.

11/10 was the 10 1/2" version of the 11/05 box. They were identical to each
             other in all aspects other than the '10s extra unibus slots.
             Memory was originally core in 4 or 8kw stacks. Most systems
             shipped with 8 or 16kw. the basic 11/05 could house 8kwcore, if
             memory serves, but a unibus expansion box could be used to add
             more. The 11/10 could house about 28kw. MOS RAM eventually
             superceded the core on these machines.
             The 11/10. 11/05 did NOT use the KA11. KD11 sounds about right,
             but I cannot remember.

11/05, 11/10 and 11/40 hit the streets at almost the same time. 11/35 very
        shortly after. 11/35 and 11/10 used the same cpu drawer and power
        supply. the 11/05, /10 provided same power as 11/20 at less cost, and
        the 11/40 increased power at same cost as 11/20.

