I absolutely agree, but why should customers have to pay extra for software
maintenance to get what they paid for originally?  I don't expect new features
for free, but I do think bug fixes ought to be available to everyone with the
right to use the software for a nominal media fee, at most.  The practice of
selling a product advertised to do "x", but charging customers extra for a
working "x" is immoral.

>(*) Before everyone flames me and tells me that this is ridiculous and
>that all versions of operating systems have bugs,

I don't expect perfection, but I do expect to get what I paid for.  When the
vendor learns of a deficiency in their product, they should see to it that
customers can get that deficiency fixed without the company profitting.

>consider what would happen if everyone actually started doing this,
>and/or if everyone started
>putting penalty clauses in those big multi-million-dollar government
>contracts for vendors that fail to fix documented bugs during a normal
>release cycle.

The latter is easier said than done, unfortunately.  The contracts I've worked
on have gone as far as requiring the vendor to supply us available patches, but
even that took some work.  And if a vendor didn't meet the requirement what
would happen?  Would they get penalized?  Would they lose the contract?  Nope,
they'd get a slap on the wrist.  We could "demand" fixes, but the fact is it
wouldn't make much difference.

