In article <1993Apr26.163723.25267@sol.UVic.CA>, zapantis@uvphys.phys.uvic.ca (Nik Zapantis) writes:
> I believe that security related questions should be answered by e-mail.

  This kind of thinking leads to what I call "ostrich syndrome" - if you
don't see it, it can't hurt you. What if one of your users sends mail to
me asking me how to get into "a system they bought used and can't access"?
In that case, you've just forced the discussion to occur where you can't
see it.

  I make similar comments to people who ask me about disabling SPAWN func-
tionality in some software I maintain - I point out how they can disable
it, and then try to explain that that *won't* fix the problem - there are
enough ways in "vanilla" VMS to get to DCL from a menu'd environment.
I further point out that DEC has a supported method to prevent this - the
"new" (as of V5.2) captive flag will terminate a process if it gets to DCL.
There's also the disimage flag will prevent the exeution of random images.

  Similarly, there is a DEC-supported method for preventing the things we
were discussing here - the locked computer room. DEC managed to get a C2
rating with this method, and I believe even Security-Enhanced VMS (SEVMS)
allows stopping in SYSBOOT.

  If you want to run open labs with conversational boot enabled, that's
fine. Just don't fool yourself into thinking it's secure, whether or not
the info exists on the net, or in the doc set, etc.

