In "Porting Your ULTRIX Application to a DEC/OSF/1 System",
it is recommended to use "lint -Q" to find pointer to int
assignments.  However, "lint -Q" does not appear to do so.
Here is a small sample program:

#include <stdio.h>

main()
{
  int i;
  int *aptr, *bptr;

  aptr = &i;
  i = (int) aptr;
  bptr = (int *) i;
  printf("First addr: %lx\n",aptr);
  printf("Second addr: %lx\n",bptr);
}

Running lint -Q on this program produces no error message concerning
the incorrect type cast.  Running the program produces the expected
incorrect result:

  First addr: 11ffffd08
  Second addr: 1ffffd08

Needless to say, "lint -Q" has not been terribly helpful
in my porting task.

