>Some of our DEC workstation have core dump in root directory periodically.
>what is the most common reason for core dump in root directory?

I couldn't say, but using the file command (i.e. typing "file /core") should
tell you what has dumped the core.

>Is there any method to prevent core dump in root directory?

For a user of the Cshell you could set a limit, i.e. "limit coredumpsize 0"
but for a daemon I don't think you can do it without altering either the source
of the program or perhaps the kernel.

I have seen a number of OS's that won't dump core if a core file already
exists.  Perhaps you could "rm /core; touch /core" or maybe create a link from
/core to /dev/null?


