In comp.sys.dec smrosen@ceres.rm.fccc.edu (Steve Rosen) writes:

>I found glitch in 'su' running under 4.3 where it accepts the typed password and
>then just hangs.  There appears to be a permission problem of some sort, but I
>can't find anything specific.  After talking with DEC C/S, their fix was to
>reboot the machine and it goes away.  This is true, but the problem always
>returns in time.  Anybody know what the permanent fix is for this?

Two possible reasons I know of: su tries to log to /dev/console and to
syslog. 
	- Every now and then /dev/console just vanishes elsewhere (after
	  kill -9 <pid of dxsession> for instance). Any process trying
	  to write to /dev/console will hang. 
	  Killing the X Server usually helps, if not: reboot.
	- If your /var filesystem is full syslog will have problems... 
	
My workaround: Keeping a suid-to-root program handy which forks your
favourite shell avoids su.  Be careful about it's permissions and don't
forget the root password after getting used to this....  ;-)

sebastian
