           THE INDUSTRY'S FASTEST DESKTOP IMAGING AND
         3D GRAPHICS SYSTEM INTRODUCED BY KUBOTA PACIFIC

SANTA CLARA, Calif., March 30, 1993 -- The fastest desktop system for imaging
and three-dimensional graphics rendering has been introduced by Kubota Pacific
Computer.

The new Kubota Kenai ("keen eye") workstations, based on Digital Equipment
Corporation's industry-leading Alpha AXP processor and Kubota's scalable Denali
graphics subsystem, offer the power to draw more than a million triangles per
second and begin with a unit priced at just $14,795 that features a SPECfp92
rating of 112.  The systems can be expanded modularly to a desk-side unit with
a SPECfp92 rating of 127.  The units establish a new industry price point and
an unmatched level of functionality and performance in imaging and graphics,
all on the desktop, according to Bruce Borden, vice president of business
development.  

As an example, a mid-range, desktop Kenai 3400/P510 with 32 megabytes of main
memory and one gigabyte of disk storage priced at $55,200 offers a SPECfp92
rating of 112 and can draw a million  Gouraud-shaded 24-bit 3D triangles per
second.  Compared to competitors' systems in that price range, the Kenai system
features twice the floating point performance of a Silicon Graphics Crimson
Extreme workstation and nearly twice the graphics performance of a
Hewlett-Packard 735-CRX48Z.

The Kenai workstations are the first to feature a desktop architecture that
combines hardware assistance for both high-performance image processing and
graphics rendering, Borden said. Typical applications include mechanical
engineering, medical and strategic imaging, computational fluid dynamics,
molecular modeling, and engineering and scientific visualization.

The Kenai systems are binary compatible with Digital's Alpha AXP OSF/1
operating system.  All software applications ported to the Alpha AXP OSF/1
environment will run on Kenai systems without modification.

The Denali graphics subsystem connects to the Kenai workstation's
100-megabyte-per-second open I/O bus and can be configured with up to six
transform engine modules.  These modules perform all floating point operations,
imaging and geometry processing in the graphics subsystem making the full
computational power of the Alpha processor available for data manipulation.  As
a result, the system can handle heavy compute loads with no degradation of
graphics performance.

In imaging applications, the Kenai workstations incorporate functionality
equivalent to or better than specialized imaging accelerators.  The Kenai
systems copy from main memory to the frame buffer at a rate of 70 million 8-bit
pixels per second, perform bilinear zooms at 20 million pixels per second and
accomplish trilinear interpolations on 8-bit 3D pixels (voxels) at a rate of 12
million per second.  

The Kenai systems use a full 64-bit implementation of OSF/1 UNIX optimized for
symmetric multiple-processor operation and are the only workstations that
support all major graphics standards including X11R5, PEX, GL4 and OpenGL,
PHIGS and AVS. 

Because Denali performance scales linearly, systems can be expanded to support
applications of advancing complexity by simply adding transform engine modules.
 Modules can be borrowed temporarily from other Kenai workstations, for
example, to configure a high-end system for a specific job that requires very
high performance.

Kubota offers the only graphics architecture with full feature support and
scalable performance across the entire product line.  A user can advance easily
from simpler 3D graphics to creating images with complex textures and volumes
and, finally, to the representation of highly complex data in fully animated
realistic images.

Because every Kenai workstation has the full feature set, users and independent
software vendors can write code for one Kenai system that will run on all
configurations.

Graphics rendering functions performed in hardware and firmware include
geometry transformation, lighting and reflectance calculations, smooth-shading
and depth-cueing interpolations, Z-buffer occlusion for representing opaque
objects, full-scene anti-aliasing (smoothing lines and edges), transparency,
texture mapping, alpha (color value) compositing and several methods of volume
data rendering.

Kenai workstations support such high-performance image-processing functions as
cine loops, image zooms, rotates and warps, convolutions, contrast stretching,
and 3D graphics and volume rending using trilinear interpretations.

A patented "Zero-Time Clear" feature implemented in Denali reduces the time
needed to change scenes by simultaneously clearing data in the frame buffer as
it is displayed.  The result is faster animation rates.

The ability to create realistic scenes is enhanced by feature orthogonality, a
Kubota-developed technique that allows advanced graphics operations, such as
texture mapping and transparency, to exist simultaneously in the same scene.

Kubota Pacific Computer, Inc., develops, manufactures and markets
high-performance image processing and 3D graphics workstations for engineering
and technical applications.  The company, headquartered in Santa Clara, Calif.,
employs 230 people.  Kubota Pacific Computer is a wholly owned subsidiary of
Kubota Corp., a highly diversified $7-billion company based in Osaka, Japan.

