kindred@telesciences.com (David L Kindred (Dave)) writes:

>I've got an OEM system containing an 11/73 processor.  Every couple of
>days, the system stops.  No errors, no red lights on the CPU or memory
>cards, no nothing.  I realize that I'm grasping for a straw here, but
>does anyone have any clues as too what to try to isolate the problem?
>I have already tried swapping cards with a functioning system and the
>problem was greatly reduced after replacing a particular DEQNA, but
>it still happens.  I'm not too familiar with the particulars of the
>Q-Bus, but I'm curious if any of the status or control lines there
>might give me a clue as to why the system is hanging?  Is there an
>easy way to tell, for instance, if a DMA transfer is locked up?  Are
>there any useful historical collections of PDP-11 troublehsooting tips
>out there in network land?

It isn't clear (at least to me) whether the system is operating for
several days at a time or if it is being powered off/on between
times when it dies.  Also, does the system die as in hang or die as
in attempts to reboot.

If the former, the following information may not help.  If the latter,
the fact that you mention the DEQNA may be significant.  DEQNA's have
a jumper/switch which causes a reboot to occur after some period of
time of network inactivity (I think this means packets sent/received
by the specific DEQNA, not the network in general).  The amount of
time it waits is programmable, I think, but defaults to a
few minutes.

Check jumper w3 on the DEQNA, it should be installed to disable the
"sanity timer".

					Megan


