In article <1r2356$hl@lll-winken.llnl.gov>, oberman@ptavv.llnl.gov writes:
|>For those who don't remember (boy, do I feel old), PDP means Programmable Data
|>Processor and these were supposedly NOT computers, because the money used to
|>design the PDP-1 was invested only for things other than building computers. Or
|>so I have read.

Nope. According to DEC's own publications, the reason they were called PDPs
instead of computers was that, at the time, computers were monstrous things
that cost millions of dollars. The idea was that by not calling it a computer,
they could avoid the "Oh my god, we can't afford a COMPUTER!" reaction and
thereby help sales.

Although it's true that for the first few years DEC was told by their
backers that they could not build computers (they sold electronic modules),
by the time the PDP-1 was designed, DEC had permission to build it.

--
