In article <1r6l2u$j5f@menudo.uh.edu>, st20e@Elroy.UH.EDU writes:
>    I have heard a LOT of bad things about DEC SCSI controllers.  Not that they
> work bad, but that they don't like third party stuff...

  There are two types of DEC-built SCSI controllers - the ones on the mother-
boards of the busless systems (like the VAXstation 3100 family) and the ones
on the bus-based systems (like the MicroVAX 3800). The motherboard-based ones
use a different driver set, and have not been qualified with non-DEC devices.
In general, you can run into problems like confusion over whether fixed or
variable block lengths should be used, and problems like the drive not being
detected unless there is media in the drive when the power-up tests run.

  The bus-based controllers emulate other DEC controllers, generally looking
like MSCP disks and TMSCP tapes. Again, DEC only tests with their devices.

>  I think CMD also makes
> a Q-bus SCSI controller.

  Yes, as well as Unibus and BI. With a 3rd-party adapter you are likely to
get better support. Also, a 3rd-party vendor can accomodate slightly oddball
devices by modifying the emulation code in the controller. For example, my
CMD controller and Archive DAT stacker looks like a TU81 drive to VMS, which
gives me compression selection via the /DENSITY switch.

> Tack an X-ibyte on that, and you won't regret the
> choice.  You can get the tape backups used in misc.forsale.workstations for
> $500- $1500 all the time. <new X-ibytes go for $3500.  It's an 8mm DAT>

  First, a clarification: 8mm is not DAT. DAT == Digital Audio Tape, which is
a 4mm format. 8mm is a video format which Exabyte has adapted for data storage.
Both are helical scan technology.

  Next, an opinion: All 8mm drives come from Exabyte, no matter who's name is
on the case. You're limited to two base transports (the older full-height and
a newer 1/2 height drive), with various electronics options. No matter how
much an integrator might like to lower the price, it's not going to go below
what Exabyte gets for the raw mechanism. There is a good deal of competition
in the 4mm market space, which keeps prices low. You can get stackers as part
of the base drive, rather than bolt-ons, as well as other features.

  I know some folks who have had some serious reliability problems with their
Exabyte drives. Quite often a drive firmware update was needed. I've been
using 4mm for some time now and I haven't had any problems. Of course, I'm
using Archive's latest high-end drive, which is certainly a lot newer than the
Exabyte 8200.

  No matter what type of drive you buy, I'd suggest you get a current model
and (if at all possible) a new unit. If your data isn't important enough to
spend the extra money on the drive, just copy it to NLA0: (or /dev/null, as
appropriate).

