In article <1reeh5INN3o1@oac4.hsc.uth.tmc.edu>, cooper@utsph.sph.uth.tmc.edu writes:
> Does anyone have any comment about DEC and CI or other possible ways
> of clustering a VAX8550 to a AXP 4610 other than via ethernet?   I would 
> appreciate you help.  Thanks in advance.

  I have no inside information on the HSC or CI product lines, but I can give
you some background which may be helpful...

  The CI operates with dual 70 megabit data paths. At the time it was intro-
duced, it was very fast. Today it's rather ho-hum. DEC has improved the various
pieces of CI hardware (compare the throughput of the CI780 with the CIXCD - in
addition to being a newer implementation, the CIXCD can also use both data 
paths of the CI for different transactions [provided some configuration issues
are met]). The CIXCD was recently revised for AXP support. One of the things DEC
obtained in the MTI settlement was the right to market MTI's CIQBA Q-bus adap-
ter.

  The HSC has been improved over time as well, with faster procesors, faster
channel cards, software enhancements, etc.

  I think anyone who is selling a CI-attached disk subsystem (MTI included) has
bought into the HSC concept (after all, any disk server on the CI is pretending
to be either an HSC or a VMS host).

  At some point, the bandwidth of the CI and the I/O-per-second rates of the 
HSC will no longer satisfy your bandwidth requirements. I can see a migration
to some new I/O bus, possibly FDDI, maybe something faster yet, for disk ser-
ving. At this point, the interim solution seems to be DSSI. Unfortunately, 
there is a _big_ performance difference between the various DSSI adapters, 
which may not be obvious when you (or your sales rep) configure systems, so be
cautious when planning.

 Also, it is unlikely that an 8550 is likely to participate in whatever new 
clustering systems DEC offers. My advice would be to move to [much] newer tech-
nology, either a 6510 or higher VAX if you're on a tight budget (maintenance
savings should be able to justify this - especially as you won't have the li-
cense "hit", being in the CSLG). If you have money to burn, go look at the new-
er 7000 series boxes, either VAX or AXP.

  Additionally, if you're coming from a 8550-range CPU, you may not need to
cluster - you can get all the performance you need in a single VMS system. So,
you'd only go clustered to serve satellites or for redundancy reasons.

