I understand your concern.
 
Indeed, before I published the description of this process in 
my column in "Digital News" about two years ago, I was quite 
concerned about the possibility that publishing the information 
might lead to a security problem.
 
While I had deduced the technique many years ago out of absolute 
necessity at a site where a system was crashing from within the 
startup procedure, I had never gotten around to seeing what TFM 
had to say on the subject.
 
Before publishing, I checked TFM, and found essentially the same 
procedure described in the perfect bound VMS System Management 
Manual.
 
My conclusions from the research were two-fold:
 
        - The procedure is of little use to a remotely located
          cracker, since it requires physical access to the 
          console. Additionally, if your console is unsecured,
          you are at little risk that you weren't already exposed
          to.
 
        - Since DIGITAL publishes the description of essentially 
          the same procedure in the manual, the cat is out of the
          bag already.
 
