David Marra (marra@padre.progress.com) wrote:
: The MicroVAX I is nolonger supported as a VAX processor.  It was 
: dropped from VMS when v5 came out (before then maybe).  It was
: basically a two board emulator of the reduced instruction set VAX
: that Dave Cutler and company out West built.  

  Well, it could be argued...Why would anyone want to slow down a nice
quick VAX with VMS anyway... :-) it gets religious, I know... They run
Ultrix-32 just fine, but they're just too damned slow for even
half-serious single-user operability.

: A MicroVAX I could hold up to 4 Mb of memory and probably supported
: (at best) the RD52 (or RD53) disk subsystems that included the RXDQ1
: or maybe RQDX2 controller.  

  You can stick an RQDX1, 2, or 3 in the box, and run up to an RD54.
Also, a KDA50 supporting various RA-series drives can be used.  Like
any other generic Q-bus processor, just about any Emulex, Dilog, or
even DEC (!) controller can be used to get some disk, tape, or serial
I/O on the machine.

: It's probably not even worth wasting the electricity to run it, but 
: then it's your electricity.

  They pull so little electricity that it's practically noise-level.
However, given the performance (or lack thereof) it's questionable
whether it's even worth THAT much.

  But, if you just want to play with it, or learn more about VAX
architecture, it DOES at least WORK.  I have some experience with
older Q-bus systems, the MicroVAX I and II in particular, so if I can
be of any assistance, drop me a line.

