> >The press release I saw put the AXP cheapie at $5000 for 100MHz. Since
> >Intel has yet to ship chips I think DEC must be well ahead of the game.
> >
> ...
> 
>   I also saw the press releases mentioning that the 3000/300L runs at 100 MHz
> and the 3000/300 runs at 150 MHz.  This can't be right, can it?  I thought
> that the 3000/400 ran at 133 MHz and the 3000/500 ran at 150 MHz.  What's 
> going on here - is this just a mistake in DEC's PR material?
> 

The biggest difference between the /300,/300L and the /400,/500 machines is the memory
interface; on a /300 and /300L, it is 64 bits wide, on the /400,/500 it is 128 bits wide.
A 21064 Alpha chip can be configured to either deal with a 64 bit or 128 bit memory
path at powerup time.  This is why the performance of the systems can be
different. So I think you can trust the PR material.  The narrower memory interface
lets us build a cheaper machine, at a cost in performance (no suprise).
			- Jim Gettys


