Could you please be more specific... If it is a backup tape it could have been
written in any one of a number of modes (The operating system was MORE THAN LIKELY
TOPS-10 or maybe TOPS-20). These were *at least* 4 ways of writing to tapes on
these systems, and all were settable. Since the machine had a 36 bit architecture
4.5 bytes were required to write a word. The problem stemmed from "what to do about
the .5 bytes" ... 
                            
Sometimes it was written as:

	4x8bit bytes followed by the trailing nybble encoded as a byte                                                  
	4x8 bit bytes, the next 4x8 bit bytes and both trailing nybbles
	4x8 bit bytes followd by the trailing and next leading nybble followd by the remaining 4x8

	this was specified by "/MODE="

This has nothing to do with "BYTE" size... which was variable on the DEC-10...
mostly 5x7 bit bytes + 1 "extra" (5 bytes and a bit ...;-) were used to store
ASCII data...

Since this O/S was (towards the end) supported at the site, and sources were available
you may be able to dig up either manuals or actual source. There has been a *major*
drive by DEC to get rid of these machines (see DECUS west this year at Vegas). They
actually may *give* you such a program if you know who to contact. (I don't). I threw
my manuals out about 3-4 years ago...

Also... make sure that these are indeed BACKUP tapes and not DUMPER format... they're
different programs. If you can find a DEC-10/20 somewhere and run an appropriate version
of CHANGE after doing a restore, you can produce tapes that a VAX can read directly.

