> Hi, all.
> 
> I have a couple of questions for anyone who is using or evaluating either
> of the low-end Alpha systems, the DEC 3000/400 or the DEC 3000/500, and
> then one about the VAXstation 4000-90 systems.
> 
> 1) Can anyone tell me what the real difference is between these two boxes?
> The DECdirect catalog says that the 3000/400 is 108.1 SPECmarks and the
> 3000/500 is 121.5 SPECmarks.  Yet, from the descriptions, the two machines
> (at least in their basic configurations) are identical.  Is it just a
> difference in the clock speed, or is there some other substantive hardware
> difference?

The two machines are (almost) identical.  One is 7/8ths the speed of the
other (both CPU clock and TURBOchannel clock).

The other major difference is the expandability; the /500 has 6 available
TURBOchannel slots.

> 
> 2) I have heard from one person doing an Alpha evaluation that while the
> units (at least the DEC 3000/400 he was looking at) are very fast in terms
> of raw computing power, they are quite lame in terms of I/O bandwidth,
> which makes them surprisingly poor performers for multi-user use.  This is
> of particular concern as we have been hoping to purchase Alpha workstations
> to use as timesharing systems.  Would anyone else care to comment on this
> facet of Alpha performance, either in general or with respect to the
> particulars of Ethernet and SCSI chips?
> 

I dunno why he was complaining about I/O performance.  TURBOchannel allows
more than 90 megabytes/second.  The SCSI chip supports SCSI II at 
~5megabytes/second; there are also IPI drives and controllers available.  
I of course have no idea how the system the person you talked to was configured.

There is also a dual SCSI adaptor (two SCSI II busses with DMA support in
a single TURBOchannel option); this brings a single /500 up to 14 SCSI busses
in a single system (there are two built into the mother board; one for internal
drives, and one on the back; then add up to six slots of the dual interface).
You can hang ALOT of disk off a system.

FDDI will run at full speed; a single /500 can transmit > 95 megabits/second
via an FDDI.


