> Does it? I didn't think the Intel x86 archetecture allowed a flat 32-bit
> address space. I don't keep up with the Intel stuff anymore, but I seem
> to recall it was limited to 16-bit offsets from one of a set of 16-bit
> segment registers. This, I believe, was the whole reason for the silly
> 640KB "main memory" restriction on the early IBM PCs.

This is true for 8086's and 8088's and 80186's, and changed somewhat under 286's.
On 80386's & 80486's it is possible to run a "virtual" 808x or a machine that has
a true "flat" 32 bit address space. MS-DOS/IBM-PC compaitibility imposes the 640
limitation 'cause that's where the memory-mapped I/O boot ROMs etc were put. A PDP-11
just can't handle a flat address space like that, NOR will NT or OS/2 ever be able
to run on an 808x (OS/2 only ran on a 8028x until IBM version 1.3). Production NT is
rumored to require 8 megs, the current Beta release wants 16... and ALPHAs are even
worse in terms of memory consumption.... A VAX on the otherhand would at least address
the issues of address space... it's no worse than an 80x86!!! (x > 2)

