The "lowest entry price systems" is a damned good point and one that I wish
DEC would take more notice of.  

The way to win against Sun's momentum is not to *just* offer better price/
performance (though that's essential), but to also offer an inexpensive
entry-point machine, at the SAME price/perf as the top-level systems. 

The other thing DEC needs to do to start regaining market share is to make
good, inexpensive tools available to software developers. (Which is really part
of the same issue -- a "low-cost entry point system" ceases to be low-cost if
the software costs are out of line.) 

If I was doing VMS marketing for DEC, the first thing I'd do would be... to get
rid of the "OpenVMS" name!  No, seriously, the first thing would be to offer a
blanket license for *all* compilers and development tools, with update service,
for, say, $250/month (including subscriptions to the ConDist/OLD CD-ROM media
and the VMS listings CDs).  

And I'd make sure that EVERY salesperson knew about that offer and that it was
as utterly hassle-free as possible.  

DEC used to have something like this (without the listings CDs), called the VMS
Developer's Portfolio or some such.  It was dropped due to poor sales. 

DEC mistook the poor sales of the Portfolio for problems with the Portfolio
concept.  The real problems were non-existent marketing at the local level (the
vast majority of customers never heard about it; worse, at least some sales
folks never heard about it, and if asked would strongly deny that it even
existed and would refuse to even try to look it up, etc., much the same as with
the VMS listings CDs today), and a lack of reasonably fast hardware to run it
on:  A Portfolio license was only good on VAXstations and MicroVAXes, and at
the time, the performance of those systems left a lot to be desired. 

So it didn't sell, and was declared a failure and discontinued. 

If I remember right, it was discontinued at just about the time that the
VAXstation 4000/VLC and VS4000/60 VAXstations were announced.  (*sigh*) 

