In article <1993Apr16.122349.709@indyvax.iupui.edu>, imhw400@indyvax.iupui.edu writes:
> In article <1993Apr14.093616.127@dallas.sil.org>, titus@dallas.sil.org (Mike Titus (Missionary Computer Jockey)) writes:
>>   The new RISC machines are using loadable
>>   microcode (or as I learned it Writable Control Store), or loadable
>>   PAL code (Alpha AXP terminology), or whatever you want to call it, just as
>>   the older machines did; 

Not really.  PALcode isn't microcode.  It's written in Alpha machine language
(macro-64, if you will).  What it does is provide a few "opcodes" at about the
level of Macro-32 opcodes (but still with Alpha's simple addressing modes),
notably thouse that require that hardware interlocks be taken out for the 
duration.  The usual use for loadable microcode (or WCS) is to interpret the
machine's regular instruction set.  That's not what PALcode does on Alpha. 

>>just like the PCs finally RE-discovering
>>   virtual memory and other things that those of us on the older machines
>>   have known for years :-).
> 
> Ever notice how much the usual descriptions of RISC principles sound like the
> Fairchild F8, or ENIAC?

Or the PDP-8!  (Named for the number of opcodes in the instruction set,
according to legend.)

