I have a VaxStation 2000 ("The 2000-Year-Old Man") running VMS 5.3 in a
Vaxcluster consisting of just this machine and a MicroVax II.  I've just
converted the VS2000 from an RD54-based to a diskless workstation (i.e., from
a boot node to a satellite node), booting off another RD54 on the MVII.  My
problem is as follows:  although the VS2000 is no longer booting off its own
RD54, nor even mounting it as a scratch disk, it can't seem to boot unless
the unused disk is present in the VS2000 and powered up.  I really want to
physically remove this RD54, because it's slowly dying; its bearings are
deteriorating and it makes a horrendous amount of noise.  My users have about
reached the limit of their noise tolerance.

So I removed the unused disk and tried to boot the VS2000, but without its
RD54 present, it doesn't even seem to go through its power-up self-tests.  I
reinstalled the RD54 and connected its data cables, leaving only the power
cable unplugged in hopes that at least it wouldn't need to be spun up, but
the result is the same; with power applied to the system, no display ever
appears on the monitor, and apparently the system doesn't go through its
self-tests.  At this stage, if I simply reconnect the RD54's power cable and
power up the VS2000 again, the system boots normally, even though I'm booting
off the ethernet and not the internal disk.  So it appears the unused disk has
to be present in order for the system to boot, or even to power up properly.

I've looked through TFM's (the VS2000 Owner's Manual and VS2000 Hardware
Installation Guide) and haven't found anything saying there has to be a disk
present in order to boot.  Does anybody know better?  Can the disk be
rejumpered or otherwise reconfigured to permit system startup without the
disk spinning?  Or does something need to be reconfigured on the VS2000's
CPU board?  Other suggestions?  Thanks in advance for your help.

