> reached the limit of their noise tolerance.
> 
> So I removed the unused disk and tried to boot the VS2000, but without its
> RD54 present, it doesn't even seem to go through its power-up self-tests.  I
[etc.]

    You can't just remove the disk from a VS2000.  It needs a "dummy load"
attached to the power connector, and I can't remember what about the
flat-cable (I'm at home now).  The dummy load is a smallish (2 1/2" by 4"
or so) printed circuit board with a bunch of resistors and a connector for
the power cable.

    Put another way: I started with a diskless VS2000 (well, 3 of 'em),
and added a Trimarchi RD54.  That involved removing the dummy load, installing
the RD54 in the box, and connecting the power and signal (flat) cables.
What I can't recall is where the flat cable started out.  In any case,
you've got to reverse the process.  

    I'm not sure where you can "officially" get a proper dummy load,
but there must be a lot of people out there who have them laying about
from when they added 3rd party disks to their VS2000's (I'm NOT one of them).

