No.  It is my understanding, that according to the current laws, DEC's
"intent" has to be backed up with hardware or software.  Your
statement does nothing to prove or disprove my statement, but simply
provides an example from a completely different field that has specific
laws in place regulating what can and can not be done; the copyright
laws clearly prohibit the example you describe.

>=Of course just because this may be technically legal, doesn't mean one
>=should go off and do it; especially if its against DEC's wishes.  
>If it's a violation of the license agreement, then it's not even "technically"
>legal.
I'm not lawyer (nor do I play one on TV :-) ), so take this with a
grain of salt.  Courts have a fairly consistent pattern of not holding
people to various "agreements" and "waivers" that they sign.  Just
because you sign a license agreement that stipulates so many users,
doesn't necessarily make it legally binding.

Another problem is what exactly determines a "user" of a system?  In
today's world of networking and X, things get rather fuzzy.

Finally, the above comments should NOT be construed to imply that I am
encouraging such behavior.  The ethics involved with doing something
that you said you wouldn't (by signing the licensing agreement),
should out weigh any potential legal loopholes.

   Dan
