titus@dallas.sil.org (Mike Titus (Missionary Computer Jockey)) writes:
>I must admit, the old French saying "Plus �a change, plus �'est la
>  m�me chose" (the more things change, the more they remain the same)
>  seems true here. The new RISC machines are using loadable
>  microcode (or as I learned it Writable Control Store), or loadable
>  PAL code (Alpha AXP terminology),

DEC really threw everybody a curve by using the term PAL code.  First,
people thought that you actually used PAL's (Programmable A? Logic) to
somehow change the AXP and make it run your OS.  Now, I see people think
that PAL is microcode.

It's not.

PAL code is Alpha assembly language code, nothing more.  It runs via a
mechanism not unlike the mode change instructions (cmuser, cmexec,
cmkrnl) in the VAX architecture.  During PAL code execution, memory
management is turned off, among other things.

See the Alpha Architecture Handbook for more information.

>or whatever you want to call it, just as
>  the older machines did; just like the PCs finally RE-discovering
>  virtual memory and other things that those of us on the older machines

True.  It's possible that newer architectures may someday abandon
virtual memory and return to some of the earlier memory management
schemes.  Memory is cheap enough that paging shouldn't have to occur on
some machines.  When workstations come standard with 1GB of RAM, and
every OS uses sharing where possible, this might just happen.

Of course, I still program my little old TI home computer.  Later this
year, I get to celebrate the 10th anniversary of TI dropping the line.
The one thing that I've noticed as machines get bigger, the programs get
bigger.  But, that's because the whole nature of the thing changes.

On the TI, if I write an editor, I have to read the keyboard myself,
write to the screen by moving things into other parts of memory, etc.
In X-Windows, the keystrokes are handed to me on a silver platter, and I
call a function and tell something else to write to the screen.  We're
getting more and more layers between the programmer and the
architecture.  Now, we have Motif and OpenLook over X.  I can write a
program there where I never worry about the keyboard at all.

Anyway, now I'm rambling.

	Darrin
