In article <1993Apr15.084457.29227@alf.uib.no>, iversen@dsfys1.fi.uib.no (Per Steinar Iversen) writes:
> Our old LN03R ScriptPrinter has died once more and this time we will not
> consider any more repairs... The most interesting replacement seems to be
> the HP LJ 4M with an Emulex NetJet interface, this card provides both TCP/IP and
> LAT support. (The HP TCP/IP card only works with supporting HPs or Suns
> somewhere on the local net.) The question is: Have anybody got a similar setup
> working with the DCPS software? Or see any reason why it should *not* work if
> we print through the Emulex LAT interface?
> 
> It is of course a problem that the DCPS software needs one or two extra licenses
> to actually work with anything but DEC printers. We have DECcampus but there 
> is no trace on the February CDs of anything like DCPS licenses. So presumably
> we will have to buy these just to find out if such a setup will work, unless
> somebody already has tried and is willing to tell us.
> 
> Any information on this is welcome.
> 

While we are using DCPS for a DEC Printserver 32 successfully, we cannot
speak for using DCPS with HP's even though we have the additional licenses.
Two main reasons for this...First, we are busy converting Xerox 4050 print
jobs/capabilities to the Printerserver 32, and secondly, we currently do not
have a HP printer that we want to solely dedicate as a postcript printer.  We
still use the HP's (100's of them) mainly in native mode.  So we have not
tried DCPS on an HP yet.  As far as you needing to buy a license to try it,
get a loaner PAK. These are pretty easy to get these days for valid reasons.

As far as the Emulex NetJet, we do have experience in that area.  We have
had 2 cards in for purchase/evaluation and have ended up sending both cards
back.  We were experiencing handshaking problems with the cards when the
printer was off-line (jobs would go to the bit-bucket) instead of "stalling"
the queue.  We are running VMS 5.5-2 and LATSYM V5.3-037.  Emulex claims this
worked fine on VMS 5.5-1, don't know the LATSYM version.  They since have
reproduced the problem, after pointing fingers our way too many times, and
supposedly have a fix.  Of course, we've heard that story too many times on
P4000 port lockups!  We never have received a workable fix for these (P4000)
yet. They have committed to bringing someone on site to look at them and
we are waiting on that to happen.  Being a 24x7 shop, it is very difficult
to keep init'ng terminal servers.  We have decided to not waste any more
effort in debugging Emulex products, so the NetJet is not in consideration
here anymore.

As far as Emulex goes, we have had positive experience with their 2501's and
have a number of them.  I don't believe we have run into any problems with
them.

As far as other vendors go, we have also looked at XCD and Lantronix.  XCD
has an internal card currently supporting LAT and TCP/IP.  It is our
understanding that IPX and Mac protocols are in development now.  We have
had positive experience in testing one of these cards.  The only thing we have
noticed is that it didn't work with TSM (Terminal Server Manager).  However,
we just received a firmware upgrade which XCD says will allow this capability.
We have yet to give it a try.

We have a Lantronix (external box) unit.  From our understanding it supports
LAT, TCP/IP, IPX, and Mac protocols.  We have done some testing with LAT
and it was favorable.  However, the printer we were using for tests (HP IV)
had to be sent to an off-site location temporarily so we have not been able
to spend much time testing it.

Again to emphasize, all of the above comments are with regards to LAT
testing only.

Kevin
