NT (which seems otherwise basically sound) is full of machine
dependencies like this.

|> It also state the reason as,
|> 
|> 	"Page faults can be expensive operations, requiring many
|>          processor cycles to complete.  Large page sizes offset 
|>          this cost, however, because more data is loaded into
|>          memory for each page fault, and therefore, fewer page
|>          faults tend to occur."
|> 
|> The VAX architecture is tied to a 512 byte page size is it not?

The VAX architecture does indeed tie you to a 512-byte page.  Which
doesn't stop VMS from clustering pages, so that you mostly get in more
than just the faulting page.  Presumably, even NT would have
difficulty in making the page-fault software entirely generic, so that
there's scope for machine dependent stuff in there.
