DECaudio started life as what we called "LoFi" at the Cambridge Research Laboratory.
(A play on words, until recently, we hadn't done the software to do HiFi!).

Here is a slightly edited version of the LoFi description.

Note that currently Digital does not have ISDN software for this option.

AudioFile will run both codec's, the telephone line interface, and the
NeXT compatible serial port.

Full programming information can be found in the AudioFile distribition available
for anonymous ftp from crl.dec.com, as the files /pub/DEC/AF/{AF2R2.tar.Z,Af2R2-patches.tar.Z,
AF2R2-other.tar.Z}.  The documents are found in the distribution
at "devices/lofi/docs/*".  
				- Jim 

Introduction
------------
 
Lo-Fi is a TURBOchannel module that will provide base hardware capabilities
for performing interesting audio experiments and research using DECStation
5000 workstations.  Since Lo-Fi is a TURBOchannel device, other workstations
supporting the TURBOchannel should have access to the same capabilities.  


Lo-Fi includes the following sections:

	Digital Signal Processor
	US Public Switched Telephone Network interface
	CODECS (analog I/O)
	Digital Audio Serial Port (with built-in 44.1 KHz Stereo DAC)
	ISDN Basic Rate Interface

In the rest of this document we describe what applications we believe 
Lo-Fi will make possible and a bit more detail about the various 
hardware sections. 

Applications
------------

With suitable software, Lo-Fi hardware makes the following applications 
possible: 

	Audio recording and playback
	Speech and audio companding
	Text-to-speech synthesis
	Speech recognition
	Answering machine/personalized message desk
	Speakerphone
	Music synthesis
	Connection to ISDN 'S' interface 
	Connection to NeXT serial port devices
	Data modem
	FAX modem



Digital Signal Processor
------------------------
 
Lo-Fi contains a digital signal processor (DSP) for off-loading 
real-time, compute-intensive activities from the host. This set of 
activities might include the vocal tract model within text-to-speech, the 
front-end analyzer within speech recognition, speakerphone echo 
cancellation, and the data pump and echo canceler within modems.
 
The digital signal processor on Lo-Fi is the Motorola DSP56001. The 
DSP is clocked by a 27 MHz oscillator and will execute a 24x24 bit 
multiply/accumulate in 74 nanoseconds.  The DSP56001 has 512 words 
of on-chip data memory and 512 words of on-chip program memory.  
Memory is expanded off-chip with a 32Kx24 bit Static RAM array.  
The DSP operates with zero wait states in the expanded memory.

Digital Audio Serial Port
-------------------------
 
The DSP56001 contains two serial ports that can be used to connect 
to an external source and sink of digital audio.  The serial port 
signals are brought out to a 15 position connector.  This 
interface is compatible to the NeXT DSP serial port, allowing 
connection to a variety of devices on the market today.
 
US and Canada RJ11 Interface (Tip and Ring)
-------------------------------------------
 
Lo-Fi includes a direct connect interface to the US and Canada Public 
Switched Telephone Network (PSTN).  Lo-Fi has been successfully tested
for compliance with FCC pt. 68 and Department of Communications Canada
regulations for connection to the public switched telephone network
when used with our device library software.  A desktop phone can be 
connected to the same line and will continue to work when the workstation
is powered off or reset.  The interface to the PSTN is known as a Data 
Access Arrangement (DAA).
 
A simple block diagram of the connections between the PSTN, desktop 
phone, and Lo-Fi is shown below.  The phone off-hook condition will 
be detected with a loop current sensing circuit.
    
                             <---- Lo-Fi | outside world-->
                                         |
	    +----------+                 |
    <-------|          |       +---------<<------ PSTN (Line)
            |  DAA &   |       |         |
            |          |    +-----+      |
            | 2-4 Wire |    |Loop |      |
    ------->| Converter|    |Det. |      |         O-----O
            |          |    +-----+      |           +-+
            |          |       |         |          /   \
            |          |-------+---------<<--------/     \        
            +----------+                 |        +-------+
                                                    (Set)        

The DAA has the following features:

o   2 to 4 wire converter hybrid
o   typical transhybrid loss of 18 db
o   ring detect circuitry (sensitivity 38 Vrms)
o   hook switch control


Dual Tone Multi-Frequency (DTMF) detection is necessary when connecting 
to the Public Switched Telephone Network (PSTN).  Although  DTMF 
detection is a trivial DSP application, it is a function that must 
run all the time.  To reduce the computational overhead and software 
complexity on the DSP, an integrated DTMF detector is used.
  
CODECS 
------

Lo-Fi has two sets of audio sources and sinks: the telephone line 
interface and the speakerphone interface.  The Main Audio Processor 
sections within two AMD 79C30A Digital Subscriber Controllers are 
used to support these two analog interfaces. The 79C30A provides 
gain control and analog filtering capabilities. The Lo-Fi analog 
ports use a fixed 8-KHz sample rate with 8-bit mu-law or A-law encoding 
(64 Kbps).

The telephone line interface transmit and receive audio connects 
to the analog port on the primary 79C30A.  The secondary 79C30A 
provides a line level and 50 ohm loudspeaker output and a line level 
and an electret microphone level input. (Please note that the mic input
is a low voltage type electret mic interface.  The supplied bias voltage
is approximately 2.5 VDC.)  

Lo-Fi supports one input and one output on each CODEC simultaneously. 
Synthesized audio (from the workstation) can be independently sent 
to the speakerphone and the phone line.  Received audio from the 
speakerphone can be sent to the phone line or to the workstation. 
Received audio from the phone line can be sent to the speakerphone 
or to the workstation.  These connections are implemented in software, 
allowing the DSP to implement mixing and echo cancelling algorithms.
 
ISDN Basic Rate Interface
-------------------------
The AMD79C30A has an integrated ISDN 'S' line interface unit. 
The Lo-Fi hardware contains the necessary components to support an 
ISDN 'S' interface.  This interface connects through an eight position 
modular jack.

 

Distribution Box
----------------

Because of the limited connector space on TURBOchannel options, a 
cable connects Lo-Fi to an external distribution box.  The distribution 
box breaks out to the following connectors:

    15 Position DSub		    DSP port (NeXT Compatible)
    5 Position Din	   	    DC Power for DSP port and Hi-Fi section
    Sub-miniature phone jack	    Mic input (mouth of handset)
    Miniature phone jack	    Line level output (Ear of handset)
    Minature phone jack	            50 Ohm Loudspeaker/output with disconnect
        (there is a small internal loudspeaker)
    Miniature stereo phone	    Headphone, DAC
    Miniature stereo phone          Line output, DAC
    4 Position modular jack	    Handset interface
    8 Position modular jack	    ISDN 'S'
    Mini-Din (2)		    44.1KhZ DAC


-- 
Digital Equipment Corporation
Cambridge Research Laboratory


