In article <1993Apr17.232817.14876@govonca.gov.on.ca>, allisom@govonca.gov.on.ca (Michael Allison) writes:
> In <bugs.735086834@netsys.com> bugs@NETSYS.COM (Mark Hittinger) writes:
> 
> I don't think Windows NT could be ported to the VAX architecture.
> According to "Inside Windows NT", Page 171,
> 
> 	"Windows NT adopts the page size established by the Intel
>          386, which is 2^12, or 4KB. (The MIPS R4000 allows 
>          software to determine the page size.)"
>
> The VAX architecture is tied to a 512 byte page size is it not?

Yes, but there's nothing to stop the operating system from always manipulating
8 pages at once; when you get a page fault, fault in the 8 page region 
containing the page you need, etc.

If you've got small pages, emulating large pages shouldn't be hard. However,
emulating small pages if all you've got is large pages is a bit tough...

