There once was a utility named DUMPER32, written by Robert McQueen and
Nick Bush of the Stevens Institute of Technology for reading TOPS-10 BACKUP
and TOPS-20 DUMPER tapes under VMS.  The earliest version I know of was
v1.0-003, which was provided to me in a distribution from Digital called
the "Integration Tools" Tape.  Both native formats were supported, as well
as the 10/20 "interchange" format.

Before our site abandoned, uh, I mean migrated from TOPS-20 to VMS in 1988,
I did quite a bit of hacking on this version to get it to work on labelled
and unlabelled v5 and v6 TOPS-20 DUMPER tapes (the original only worked on
v3 and v4 unlabelled DUMPER tapes), and tried not to break the TOPS-10 BACKUP
processing.  I did test it on unlabelled BACKUP tapes written under TOPS-10
v6.03, but that was as good as I could do for TOPS-10 testing.   The code
from Stevens was modular and cleanly written, so it was not very difficult
to keep away from the TOPS-10 BACKUP code.  My version would also correctly
restore files split across multiple tape volumes, and would automatically
remove line sequence numbers from text files (Blecch.  Remember those?)

Anyway, this was sent to DECUS and was made available on the Spring 1988
VAX SIG tape (VS0076).   The VMS BACKUP saveset I sent to DECUS is also
available by anonymous FTP from this site (indyvax.iupui.edu, 134.68.1.2)
in the directory [PUB.VMS] in the file DUMPER32.BCK-Z.  This is a compressed
VMS BACKUP saveset that you can uncompress with the LZDCMP.EXE image in the
same directory.  Executables, object files, and Bliss-32 source files are
all included in the saveset.  You must transfer both files in binary mode.

Please note that this software is provided as-is -- I cannot provide support
for it, nor can I help with problems with individual tapes.  You *must*
know the blocking factor of the tape to read it with this thing; it is not
very smart but it got the job done for us.  Just last month I had to dust it
off to read a 12-year-old TOPS-10 BACKUP tape someone had squirreled away...

Historical note: TOPS-10 and -20 fans may remember the funny three-cornered
Integration Tools symbol: it was like something out of an Escher print that
could not exist in physical reality -- of course this gave one immediate warm
and fuzzy feelings about TOPS/VMS "integration" (which was soon replaced by
the more appropriate term "migration" :-).
--
