|>32, or at least 32?  Does anyone know if NT on Alpha will use 64-bit or 32-bit
|>pointers, or if it will allow both but prefer one or the other?

At the moment 32, not at least 32.  Several things in the Windows 32 API would
have to be widened to accomodate 64-bit addressing.  According to Microsoft,
they have been careful when implementing NT not to preclude larger address
spaces than 32 bits in the future, but it is not supported at present.  They
say that they will support 64 bits if and when there is market demand for it.

Alpha NT is a 32-bit system, with a 32-bit address space and 32-bit pointers
(except of course for some bits of the kernel, since the underlying machine is
64 bits).  Nothing would prevent a user application from using 64-bit pointers,
but the top 32 bits would always be zero, and you have to use 32-bit pointers
when talking to the operating system, so there's little point.

--PSW


