|> DEChub 90 is a really nice piece of engineering, and we already have a
|> few installed in our local network.  I maintain, though, that the thing
|> would have been infinitely more useful if, instead of its thin-wire
|> (10base2) connection to the backbone Ethernet, it had had a thick-wire
|> (10base5) one.  The way it is now, if one wants to hook up a DEChub,
|> and the backbone is thick-wire, then one has to bend over backwards
|> and install a DESPR (single port repeater) plus a short stretch of
|> thin-wire.  If thereafter you want to string a series of thin-wire
|> stretches from the DEChub, then you're already at the 2 repeater
|> limitation of Ethernet.
|> 
|> Anyone know why DEC did it this way ?  Anyone know if such a beast
|> as a DEChub 90 with thick-wire connector to the backbone might exist
|> or be in planning ?


The ThinWire port was added to the side since the modules communicate with each other via "thinwire" on the backplane.  The real intention is so that 2 hubs can be daisy chained together.  the serial management can be daisy-chained as well with a BC16 cable.  A DECBridge 90 plugged into hub #1 will see the modules in it's hub as modules 1-8 and the modules in the daisy chained hub (#2) as modules 9-16. 


If we were to provide an AUI port on the side of the hub then there would have had to have been a repeater built in.  according to 802.3 rules a repeater is needed when ever you change media types.  If we had wired the backplane internally to look like a DELNI then it would have been a problem radially wiring etch to the connectors, instead of a bus.  each of these solutions would have required active electronics on the backplane, something we didn't want to do.

We are offering a product called the DEFAR.  It's a DEChub 90 module that has a fiber and AUI port on the front for a list price of $525.  it can also be purchased with a standalone power supply for $660 and used as a DESPR.

no version of a DEChub 90 has been or will be produced that has an AUI port on it.  the DEChub 900 does not have one either


