In <1q9b7p$cng@nigel.msen.com> msage@garnet.msen.com (Micro Sage Software) writes:
>[...]
>Nowadays, DEC offers SCSI periperal interconnections and supports
>networking standards like TCP/IP, POSIX compliance, etc.  They have been
>looking at the outside world for a few years now and it's showing with the
>offerings they are making.
>[...]

Only because they were forced into this kicking and screaming.  They
STILL don't have client NFS support in VMS (third parties have had it
for a LONG time), and their SMTP mailer is really bad.  The POSIX
shell is an add-on that many VMS poeple don't install because they
don't know what it is or are afraid of it.

Sure things are getting better, but they still have a LONG way to go.
And the speed of the AXP boxes is even getting me thinking about using
some VMS (no OSF/1 on our AXPs) so that I can get lighting fast
compiles and links (compiling a Motif client takes eons on a
DECstation).  And I LOVE Unix...

   Dan
