NNTP-Posting-Host: carson.u.washington.edu

I just ported a C program that computes a 3-D Delauney triangulation from
my 4000/90 to a 3000.  I tried both VEST (no parameters) and recompilation.
It is a very compute intensive program with both a lot of structure following
and floating calcs.  I won't try to format nice benchmark numbers but 
the results were that a 20 second run (CPU) on the 4000/90 took only 7 secons
on the 3000 AXP.  The VESTed code took 2 minutes - explained by the
attempt to use default VAX floating point!  I don't know how to make VEST
allow native floating, but might try.  Recompilation was simple - 
CC/STANDARD=VAXC worked like a charm.

My 2 cents: the darn thing is for real!  And this was indeed production code.
I won't try to speak for I/O intensive.

ed


  
