In the interest of historical trivia.

The UWA PDP-6 was the first time-sharing system sold in the world. It was
installed in late 64/ early 65. In 1971 it configuration was:

166 CPU, serial number 4
162 fast memory
3x 163C, 16KW core memory boxes. Sn1 and 4 and ?
8x 555 Microtapes
551 Microtape controler.
630 system with 32 half duplex lines ( 2 wire 50ma )
461 card reader controler, plus a Burrows card reader.
760 paper tape reader
346 display with 370 light pen
646A line printer controler
136 data channel, on port 0 of the memory for disks
TM-10B tape controler, with 4 7 track transports.
ka-10 type data channel to port 1 for the tape.
Custom interface to PDP-8
PDP-8, 4KW mem. Disk controler.
2x 646(??) CDC disk drives.
Some sort of interface to a IR spectrometer??
Interface to cyclotron??

Not at all sure on the last. I will see if I can contact some people
who build it.

The front panel and CPU cab are at the 'Wireless Hill' mueseum in here
in Perth. I had lots of the 6-10 memory cable, plus a 6 IO bus cable,
but all but 2 mem cable were destroyed in a fire. The 6 memory boxes
were in use on a KA-10 that replaced the 6 till 1980. They then went,
from memory, to the university of Queensland.

The KA was replaced by a KL-10E in 1980, and in 1990 I purchased it for $2.
512 KW MG-20 mos, RP-06, 4xRP07. Not yet operational due to fire. The room its
in needs its ceiling ripped out and fixed properly. It is intended that it
WILL run again, and be back on the net one day. Phoenix.cma.org.au. :-)
If you wonder about the name, look over the old ( early 80s ) LCG posters.
Note the one on Boston harbour. :-)

~Paul



