In article <1993Apr13.173958.81363@embl-heidelberg.de> omond@embl-heidelberg.de (Roy Omond) writes:
>
>DEChub 90 is a really nice piece of engineering, and we already have a
>few installed in our local network.  I maintain, though, that the thing
>would have been infinitely more useful if, instead of its thin-wire
>(10base2) connection to the backbone Ethernet, it had had a thick-wire
>(10base5) one.  The way it is now, if one wants to hook up a DEChub,
>and the backbone is thick-wire, then one has to bend over backwards
>and install a DESPR (single port repeater) plus a short stretch of
>thin-wire.  If thereafter you want to string a series of thin-wire
>stretches from the DEChub, then you're already at the 2 repeater
>limitation of Ethernet.
>
>Anyone know why DEC did it this way ?  Anyone know if such a beast
>as a DEChub 90 with thick-wire connector to the backbone might exist
>or be in planning ?

I think the intent of the DEChub for company-wide backbone connections
was to plug in a DECbridge 90, which has a thick-wire port.   Yes, it costs
more that way, but if you don't have a big network you shouldn't pay for
the bridge.  If you do have a bid network you probably NEED the bridge to
keep your backbone from being too congested.

The thin-wire connector on the edge of the DEChub 90 is really intended for
hooking up a second DEChub 90, not your backbone.

