After reading all of the PDP-10 stories, I just thought I'd toss out
  this bit of trivia:  Anybody know the origin of the floppy?

My understanding is that IBM originally developed the 8-inch floppy
  as a load device for microcode on the old Series 1.  If I'm wrong,
  I trust that someone who knows the truth will set the record
  straight.

I must admit, the old French saying "Plus �a change, plus �'est la
  m�me chose" (the more things change, the more they remain the same)
  seems true here. The new RISC machines are using loadable
  microcode (or as I learned it Writable Control Store), or loadable
  PAL code (Alpha AXP terminology), or whatever you want to call it, just as
  the older machines did; just like the PCs finally RE-discovering
  virtual memory and other things that those of us on the older machines
  have known for years :-).

