>There was actually a -10 at DEC, (one of the development machines) that
>had a 680 on it quite late.  We had real trouble bringing up one of the
>later monitors on it (7.00?) after some mods.  It seems that the IO
>command that started the 680 had been re-used (rewired?) and was now
>used for a cache sweep; instant crash; that one took a while to find,
>of course the mneminics in the assembly code didn't look at all alike.

Sounds like one of Grace Hopper's stories about the early days of
the industry (she described herself as "the third programmer").
Programmers would work on their projects during the day, and the
engineers would come in at night and change the hardware in a manner
which invalidated the carefully-designed programs.

Sort of like what happens today when the {DOS, MacOS, UNIX, etc} system
gets upgraded and your favorite program no longer works...except that
it happened on an even shorter time scale.

Joe Morris / MITRE


