In article <1993Apr9.062105.25617@colorado.edu> ghill@dot.cs.colorado.edu (Gregory Hill) writes:
>Does anyone know what the distinction between a "signalling NaN" and a
>"non-signalling NaN" is? In Gerry Kane's "mips RISC Architecture" book,
>the distinction is mentioned but I cannot find any details about what
>this actually means.
>
>thanks, greg hill
>
The distinction is defined in the IEEE-754 floating point standard.
Signalling NaNs cause invalid operation exceptions when they are used as
operands.  Quiet (non-signalling) NaNs simply propogate another quiet NaN
to the result of the operation and go on.


