: > 

: Correct and wrong. Two cabs...

The first -10 frontend was a PDP-8/I.  No UARTs.  Not
even a crummy shift register.  The code did the _bit_ timing
both send and receive.  But back in the bad old days, a
bit lasted a little over 9 millisecs.  Heck, you could
execute a couple thousand instructions in that time.  {8^)

If memory serves, it was a guy named Kaufman that founded
DCA (Data Communications Associates, the IRMA folks).  One of
their first products was a higher-performance replacement
for DEC's code in the -8.  Then I think they made some
"shift register" boards for it. 

Twenty years ago today...


