In article <1993Apr10.024524.22652@cunews.carleton.ca>
	gfrajkor@superior.carleton.ca (George Frajkor) writes:

>   What is the accepted difference between a workstation and a PC in
>the first place??

Let's back up to 1986.  Suppose you're looking for a machine to run in your
lab.  You may want to do CAD, or image processing, or some other graphics
intensive application.

If you buy from DEC or Sun you get a megapixel display with a large monitor,
with color and accelerated graphics if you want it, 10 megabytes or more of
memory if you want it, hundreds of megabytes of disk space.  You get a
multitasking operating system (UNIX or VMS) which supports all this
hardware.  You get software and support.  Networking, if not standard, is
easily available.

_Now_ you can get all those things on PCs.  Not then, at least not easily,
and not nearly as cheaply as today.

You might also want to think of "workstation" as being in contrast not to
PCs, but to larger systems which they often replaced.

You can point out a lot of differences which have disappeared.  For example,
I could put 256 MB of memory into my 80486 system at home, and several
gigabytes of disk.  On the other hand, my system, while my 66 Mhz 80486 is
the leader of the pack in the PC world, it is a lot slower than the fastest
workstation.

Use, not hardware capability, has become a more important factor.

--
    John Carr (jfc@athena.mit.edu)


