This brings up a real question of just what is open.  To tell the truth, I
used to be a DEC die-hard.  All we had was DEC equipment and if DEC didn't
have it, we didn't want it.  About 3 years ago, when it came time to
migrate our applications from the trusty PDP-11 to something new, the
first people we approached were DEC.  They basically told us to go away
(we are a small company).  Instead we went with the DG AViiON line (their
new RISC based, UNIX based line).  These have turned out to be very nice
systems and have helped us to realize that there is more out there than
just DEC.

Now with that aside, I still think DEC has a much more comprehensive
software offering than most others.  I used to look down on UNIX as a
university graduates toy (unstable, cryptic, no attention given to the
real world, etc).  The UNIX we have here, however, is rock solid.  I
haven't had a crash in 2 years (excepting hardware problems).  The cryptic
part can still be argued, however. 

So why is it in everyone's mind the Open != DEC?  I think it's because of
DEC's past, not it's present or future.  In the past, the only thing that
could connect to a DEC machine was other DEC machines (ala DECNet,
MSCP/SDI, BI Bus, etc).  Whenever someone tried to do that, DEC sued the
bejeezers out of them.  This left many people feeling that if they chose
DEC, that was it.  Your stuck.

Lets also not forget Uncle Ken's "Unix is SnakeOil" statement.  That one
has haunted DEC for a while.

Nowadays, DEC offers SCSI periperal interconnections and supports
networking standards like TCP/IP, POSIX compliance, etc.  They have been
looking at the outside world for a few years now and it's showing with the
offerings they are making.

So, to try to sum this up (probably not possible): Unix was the first
generally accepted 'Open' system.  DEC didn't like the Unix/Open concept
for a long time.  Peoples opinions were made based on this.  DEC has
opened up quite a lot now.  People need time/education (read: marketing)
to realize this and beleive it to be the truth, not just a cry from a
company struggling to keep up (as perceived from the outside world). 

Sorry if this post is kinda rambling, but I just woke up, Sunday Morning,
bit of a hangover.  Coherence is probably not my strong suite right now.

