was a communications concentrator) could be purchased and stuck 
on its I/O bus.  The PDP-6 microtapes were not on a front-end, 
but integral to the graceful brushed aluminum and painted metal console 
with its "high speed" papertape reader and rows of microswitches 
and blinken-lights.  Booting the machine involved (1) keying in
the instructions to the PTR on the microswitches, (2) loading the
papertape reader, and (3) depressing the run lever.  Front end?
We don't need no stinkin'...



