	Those are all good points. VMS has one of the most impressive
collections of standards-compliant stuff of any operating system out there.
If you accept "Open" == "Standards Compliant" there is no question but
that VMS rules. I suspect a lot of people conflate "Openness" with
"Inexpensive" and tend to perceive VMS' extremely high licensing costs
with being "Proprietary" - after all, the logic goes, if it wasn't all
vendor-specific, it'd be cheaper.

	If I were running a financial institution, I'd probably do it
on VMS, or MVS because there's loads of application support in the
environment. But VMS and MVS are going to remain niche markets because
they're so expensive - developing all those zillions of interfaces and
APIs "system" refers to costs money and someone has to pay for it.

	VMS and MVS are both demographically flat right now - the VMS
and mainframe populations are flat, possibly turning downward slightly.
UNIX is still growiing, but I predict it'll taper off in another 5
years and then it'll be in the same mode as VMS is in today: it'll have
its advocates claiming it's still the best - but nobody'll care anymore.

