dec binder with ( As modified for the University of Western Australia ) in
the title page.

~Paul

PS The 8 in question is still around as well. I hve a copy of the code that
it ran. Was a fun system. One problem was that the 8 could not do a divide
quickly enough ( for converting block number to cylinder number? ) so
it would load up the numbers into the interfsace to the 6 and interupt it
on channel 6. This was a 'reserved for doing divides for the 8' interupt
level. So you could say that the 6 was one of the first machine to have an
intelegent loadable IO controler ( the 8 ) and thst the 8 was one of the
first to have a 'FPU' ( the 6 ). :-)



