A friend of mine recently did some performance testing of HSC9X-SA (SCSI 
cards) and RZ26's, and asked me to post this for him.

I didn't do this testing, but I can forward questions/comments to him.

-dan
 dwing@uh01.colorado.edu

-----

Disk drive evaluation of HSC9X-SA (SCSI) and RZ26

It was found that the RZ26 while listing excellect statistics, performed
extremely poorly, and in most cases worse than an RA92.

In particular, with 8 block request sizes we could only get 41 I/Os per
second out of the RZ26. The performance worsened when multiple drives were
running in the same BA350 cabinet. With multiple drives accessing we saw
no more than 30 I/Os per second.

This 30 I/Os per second statistic is close to what DEC states in the
cluster configuration guide. They state 31 I/Os per second, whereas they
state 53 I/Os per second for a RA92. We found this to be a reasonable
comparison. In short seek I/Os (400 megabytes and less) the RA92 actually
had a faster access time than the RZ26. Only with longer seeks did the
RZ26 show any gain at all.

The most surprising test was the spiral benchmark test. In this test a
RA92 does approximately 1,700 KB per second. The RZ26 did only 830 KB.
This has significant impact on backup and disk to disk copies. Several
Beta sites found this issue to be extremely important.

Based on the above we did some further tests of the interface itself. We
found that the HSC9X-SA is the bottleneck. The interface will only do 180
I/Os per second. Additionally we found that it was adding about 6
milliseconds to every I/O. This explained the reason for the slowness of
the RZ26 access.

Further checking of the interface and the drive showed us that there is no
disk drive optimization taking place. Both Seek Reordering and Rotational
Position Optimization are standard HSC DSA features, and they are not
being performed by the HSC9X-SA/RZ26 combination. This makes their
performance deteriorate far quicker than other disk drives that implement
the standard drive optimization techniques.

Other issues that we noticed, were that the BA350 did not fit well into
the top of the HSCs. In fact, the instruction manual told us to bend the
brackets to get it to fit. The unit number is not either set or displayed
on the front of the drive. We had to manually lablel the drives. The unit
number could only be set from the HSC console, which we didn't
particularly like.

We did try to plug in a standard SCSI drive to the HSC9X-SA to see if it
was really standard SCSI that was being supported on the HSC. Please don't
try that, it made our HSC crash, leading us to the conclusion that it
really isn't OPEN at all.

Based on our tests with the Digital News and Review benchmark, our own
benchmarks, and application testing, we have found that the HSC SCSI card
is a very poor performer and represents a step backwards.


