In article <1993Apr10.024524.22652@cunews.carleton.ca> gfrajkor@superior.carleton.ca (George Frajkor) writes:
>
>   What is the accepted difference between a workstation and a PC in
>the first place?? I am never clear on this.

A PC is a personal computer that you have sitting at home on your desk
for personal stuff.  A workstation is a machine stationed somewhere at
work where _work_ is done. :-)

Seriously, I don't see an either/or here.  A PC _was_ marketed as a
computer where personal things can be used on, like simple word processing,
check balacing, etc., etc.  Similarly, a "workstation" was marketed as a
powerful machine that can do heavy load work for CAD, 3-D simulation,
server, and so forth.  Now PCs are approaching the power of low-end
workstations.  In fact, PCs are being used in what was traditionally
workstation-type work.  There is no reason why an Intel-based computer
cannot be a workstation.

I think the people who are separating PCs and workstations into two 
distinct groups are really splitting hairs.  So, IMO, only the way it
is used defines whether a computer is a PC or a workstation.

>  Now that you can buy
>Ataris and Amigas with Unix, and boost their memory capacities and
>disk space to infinity, why are there distinctions being drawn??

Depends on whether you are running Tetris or 500,000 point 3-D
simulation on it.

> When
>I connect my Commodore 64 through a VT102 emulator to the Carleton
>University SUN network, am I a workstation??

No, you are a terminal.  Workstations are used as servers.  If I
used a Sparc 10 to connect to a network, it is a terminal, too, while
it is being used as such.

All just my opinion, of course.

James.

