>   What is the accepted difference between a workstation and a PC in
>the first place?? I am never clear on this.  Now that you can buy
>Ataris and Amigas with Unix, and boost their memory capacities and
>disk space to infinity, why are there distinctions being drawn?? 

A person from the business community will likely tell you there is no
difference, however, a technical person may see a difference depending 
on what they are doing.

The biggest difference I see, as a somewhat technical person, is that
the internal bandwidth of workstations (high end ones at least) is much
greater than for PCs. Much of the extra cost is probably eaten up 
providing these higher speed interconnects (and marketing them of course)

Some of the lower cost "workstations" use the same interconnects as high
end PCs, so what you are really getting here is a PC with a supported
_real_ os (ie. not MS-DOS/MS-WINDOWS).

