In article <Mar30.090934.17247@roxi.rz.fht-mannheim.de>, andre@borneo.dkfz-heidelberg.de (Andre Schroeter) writes:
|>
|>then i tryed to add xdm in rc.local (the manual for the vxt 2000) says to
|>do so. but this does not work. xdm doesn't start at boottime !!!
|>
|>so my questions are:
|> - how to autostart xdm at boottime (only for the vxt)?

I've got xdm running from my rc.local file and it works fine. 

Here's the fragment of my rc.local file that does the trick.
*********
# 
# start xdm to manage X displays
#
[ -f /usr/bin/X11/xdm ] && {
	/usr/bin/X11/xdm & echo -n ' xdm'			>/dev/console
}
echo '.'							>/dev/console
#
# Removing Opser remnant files
#
echo 'Removing remnant Opser files' > /dev/console
*********

/usr/bin/X11 is not on the PATH at the time rc.local is called, so you have to explicitly
qualify the executable's name

|> - if no other possibility use xdm at the decstation also.

You can use xdm at your DECstation (that's how I do it). But you don't
have to. If you aren't using xdm for the local display, then make sure that
there is not an entry for your local server included in /usr/lib/X11/xdm/Xservers. If you
want to use xdm for your DECstation, then make sure an entry is in /usr/lib/X11/Xservers for
the local display, and turn off the entry in /etc/ttys that starts Xprompter.

|> - is there a way to have a chooser menu on the vxt berfore login
|>   to choose the host ?? we have 3 decstations and one RS6000.

Yes. First, don't put entries into /usr/lib/X11/xdm/Xservers for the VXT's. Second, don't 
auto-start an IP X session from your VXT. Third, to start a IP X session, select an IP X
session from the VXT's Terminal Manager window. It will bring up a dialog box asking
you for a host to start the IP X session to.

I hope you are running VXT software Version 1.2 or later. Older revisions did not work well
with xdm.


