/* ---------- "Only 2 log-ins allowed -- Why?" ---------- */
I've been taking care of our decstation 3100 (running ultrix 4.2)
for a while now, and I've always wondered why is it that the
machine only allows 2 users to be logged on at the same time.

I think it has to do with our site license, but I'm not sure.
Can anyone explain this to me, and perhaps offer suggestions on
how I can change the number of log-ins to the number of network
ttys (as it should be for any old unix box)?

Thanks in advance!
-- 
-- Andrew Duchowski                        |                                  --
-- Texas A&M University                    |  This page intentionally blank   --
-- andrewd@cs.tamu.edu (non-NeXT mail)     |                                  --
-- andrewd@visual2.cs.tamu.edu (NeXT mail) |                                  --
/* End of text from tisdec.tis.tandy.com:comp.sys.dec */

DEC stringently adheres to the licenseing policies of their contract with USL.

Most unix-based systems can be licensed for different user level's:

	1-2
	1-4
	1-8
	1-MANY

with the PC based variants opting for 1-2, with 1-many as an upgrade.

The old BSD release systems did not have this user restriction, but USL
wants its cut of the userload. (It could be interpeted that USL was condesending
enough to drop the LICENSE fee for smaller systems.)  The per user license
appears to have appeared with SYSTEM V.

ALL DEC-RISC systems come with a 2 user license (the more powerful servers
usually include an upgrade to 4 or 8 users).  To increase the number
of concurent users on this system you need to have the purchasing agent
at your site contact the DEC rep concerning buying a 3-4, and posibly a 5-8
user License (you must purchase each intervening level).

