|>I've been taking care of our decstation 3100 (running ultrix 4.2)
|>for a while now, and I've always wondered why is it that the
|>machine only allows 2 users to be logged on at the same time.

ULTRIX (please, it's in all caps because it's a trademark) manages the number
of simultaneous users via the License Management Facility (lmf). login has
been re-written (as have some other parts of ULTRIX) to check the license
database for a free license before completing the login. ULTRIX comes with
a two user license out of the box. Yes, even network ttys are covered in 
this scheme. The third user to log in will find that all available licenses
are taken, and will be denied access.

Ouch. I can feel the flames now. 

It appears that Digital more strictly defines what a "user" is under ULTRIX,
either by our interpretation or as a explicitly stated part of our licensing 
agreement with AT&T, I don't know which. Yes, I know that other vendors don't do 
this. 

The Digital "legal way" of dealing with this is to acquire licenses for the 
additional users beyond 2. This is an exercise that is left to the reader 
to determine whether or not the licenses have to be purchased, or if they 
covered under some other agreement.

There may be other ways of dealing with the situation (I'm sure that someone
will suggest replacing portions of ULTRIX), but that could be construed as
a violation of your license agreeement. I know this point is and has been arguable,
but I have to make the statement anyway - something about biting the hand that
feeds you, if you catch my drift.


