kellym@tekgen.bv.tek.com (Kelly Mc Connell) writes:
>From my trusty UDA50 Field Maintenance Guide:
>The one with the least amount of buffering should be placed at the front of
>the bus.  The heavily buffered UDA should be placed at the end of the UNIBUS.

>A jumper inserted on the M7485 module changes the average number of UDA NPR
>requests over a given amount of time by delaying a request for 0, 6.2, or 10
>microseconds (see table below).
>	0  	T4-T6
>	6.2	T5-T6
>	10	T6-T7
>So, put the RL at the front of the bus, put the UDA at the end of the bus, and
>set the 10 usec delay on the UDA to decrease it's pig factor.

I disagree with this conclusion: Putting the RL11 controller at the
front of the bus should be enough to allow the RL11 to do DMA whenever
it wants.  Remember that the NPG signal is daisy chained through all
of the DMA devices, starting with the ones in front.  Now if the UDA50
requests again quickly, the new grant is going to go right through the
RL11, so it will be able to use the grant if it needs it.  Thus, there
shouldn't be any need to slow the time between UDA 50 requests, it is
already very low priority due to being so far back on the bus.  If you
do run into problems, you might try the 6.2 usec setting, but I think
that 10 usec is excessive, unless you have a real-time peripheral that
is losing interrupts, but then that's another story.  Otherwise, at 10
usec extra delay per transfer, you'll take 5 msec extra for every 1kb
that you get off of the UDA.  That adds up fast!

Arrange devices by latency tolerance (buffering) but then allow them
to run as fast as they can, when they are able to.

