Subject: Hmmm, surprising numbers on a 3000/500 vs 4000/90
Message-ID: <1993Apr9.005037.4908@coe.montana.edu>
Sender: usenet@coe.montana.edu (USENET News System)
Organization: CS
Date: Fri, 9 Apr 1993 00:50:37 GMT
Lines: 47



M1:DEC VAXstation 4000/90, VMS 5.5-2, 64MB's RAM, 2 RZ25's in a stripe set.

M2:DEC 3000/500, OpenVMS 1.0, 96MB's RAM, 1 RZ26.

(Disk drives listed are ones being used, not total config. None are
system disks).

Running some tests of our software to kind of get a feel for performance.

The program in question does a fair amount of I/O, not too much CPU.  It's
written in FORTRASH.  Uses only about 2000 pages of memory on a VAX.

Machines totally idle except for me running this test.

LD == Local disk (be it stripeset or single disk)
RD == DECRAM RAMDISK  (10,000 blocks)

					Elapsed time  |  	CPU usage
M1->LD :    06:18:26         00:39:00
M1->RD :		00:38:55				 00:30:37 (Obviously the RAM disk helps)
-
M2->LD :		02:01:43         00:27:18  Interesting. No stripe, still faster
M2->RD :    00:48:47         00:26:03  ???

I would've suspected better improvement on the ALPHA.  One thing to note 
that on M1, a mon sys showed consistently that I/O's were at 300-310/second,
but on M2, barely over 100, and peaked at 120.  (when m1 and m2 were writing
to the RAM disk)

I am curious about the 3x improvement in speed over accessing the stripe set.
I would've expected the I/O improvement from the stripeset to keep it ahead
of the single RZ26, but apparently not.

I am disappointed in the RAMDISK timings however.  i would've expected the
Alpha to come very near to 30 minutes of Elapsed time, rather than 48.

I suspect that something's a little off with the RAM disk driver on the
Alpha, and my configuration.  Perhaps I could've tuned the system better
to improve this performance.

