In article <C512Bw.8t7@dove.nist.gov>, bagwill@swe.ncsl.nist.gov (Bob Bagwill) writes:
> We have a GPX that won't boot off its ra0.  At one point the boot
> sequence stopped at: 2..
> 
> The "ready" light for that drive would go off at that point.  After
> turning it off, and letting it rest, it would count down to zero, then
> get an error.
> 
> When I type "B DUAO", it says the device is offline.

  The fact that the ready light is going out implies (ta-da! 8-) that the drive
dropped ready. Now, this can only happen from 3 things - a spin-down command
from the host (which I _know_ the boot ROM doesn't emit), operator intervention
by pressing run/stop, or a drive fault.

  My best guess would be that your fault lamp is burned out or missing. Try
snarfing one from one of the other RA8x's (these *are* RA8x's, right?) and 
using it in this drive. If fault comes on, push the fault button and you'll
get a code displayed on the front panel lamps. Mail me the code and I'll tell
you what it means.

  If you're feeling adventurous, open the top hatch of the RA81 (there's a 
small Allen screw on the top [front right] which you turn 1/3 turn to open the
topp). Do this with the power off as the cover tends to drop pieces of metal
on the logic boards. Once you have the cover up, on the right hand side you
will see an RS-232 connector. You'll need a terminal set to 300 baud. Power up
the drive (with terminal not connected to drive, or the drive will come up
comatose), make sure both A and B front panel buttons are out, hook up the ter-
minal and type ^C. You'll get an RA8x> prompt. Type RUN DIAG. It should pro-
gress through various tests. Now press the front panel RUN/STOP button. The
drive will say "Front panel function in progress" on the terminal. After it
comes up and reports the test results, say RUN DIAG again. It should find the
problem and report the most likely defective component (probably the HDA). If
it doesn't find the fault, leave the terminal connected, push in the A or B
button as appropriate, and try your install again. When the drive goes offline,
release the A or B button, type ^C on the terminal to wake up the drive, and
say SHOW DIAGNOSTIC FAULTS. You'll get a list of fault codes. Mail 'em to me
and I'll decode them for you.

