In Article <C4t9nA.GG6@helios.physics.utoronto.ca>
sysmark@helios.physics.utoronto.ca (Mark Bartelt) writes:
>Here's a question that should be easily answerable by any DECie familiar
>with the OSF/1 ethernet driver and/or the ethernet hardware on the Alpha
>boxes, but which wasn't answered by any of the online documentation that
>we had on our evaluation unit (and I'm not certain myself whether it's a
>hardware or driver issue) ...
>
>We're about to take delivery of a 3000/400S and a 3000/500S, and I'd like
>to know whether we should configure the transceivers for "SQE on" or for
>"SQE off".
>
>I know there are some vendors' systems that want SQE, and that there are
>others which get confused if SQE is enabled, and that still others don't
>care.  Which category are the AXP systems in?

If an end system wants SQE off, it is NOT 802.3 compliant. Go complain to the
vendor. If some sales critter TELLS you that it should be off, don't believe
it.

That said, most Ethernet drivers don't bother to check for SQE errors, so it
doesn't make any difference. DEC boxes do. DEC has people who actually
understand the spec writing drivers.

As per 802.3, SQE must always be ON except for repeaters. Please understand
that some repeaters are called hubs. 10BaseT hubs are repeaters, so that if
they have an "up link" AUI connector, the MAU on that line should have SQE
turned off.

I am aware of one case of a workstation that had a software error in the
Ethernet driver that required SQE to be turned off. This has long since been
fixed.

Bottom line: Always turn the SQE on for any "terminal" equipment. This includes
workstations, bridges, routers, or anything that does not extend the "collision
domain" (802.3 talk) of the network. If SQE is off you have no way to learn of
a potentially disaterous failure of the collision detect circuit in the MAU.
While this is a rare event, the hours of troubleshooting it can take to isolate
without SQE makes it worth doing right.

R. Kevin Oberman, SQE Evangelist	Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory
